Keep the Ro sessions waiting for their delayed delete on a timer list of their own.

A terminated Ro session is put back on the timer for 120 s before it is
deleted (put_ro_session_on_wait()). Every Ro timer shares one sorted list
under one lock, and insertion walks it from the tail to the first entry
that is not later than the new one. The waiting sessions are the latest
entries on the list most of the time, so the credit timer of a call being
answered (dlg_answered(), reserved seconds minus the timer buffer) walks
past all the sessions that ended in the last ~95 s.

That is 2 Ro sessions x 120 s of the call rate: about 30000 entries at
127 calls/s, walked by every answer, under the lock every BYE and every
answer of every SIP worker needs. Sampling the S-CSCF's four SIP workers at
150 calls/s offered found 33 of 48 stacks in insert_ro_timer(), 29 of them
waiting for its lock, and the S-CSCF capped at ~127 calls/s with none of
its processes above a quarter of a core.

All waits have the same interval, so on a list of their own every insert
lands at the tail and the credit-timer list holds the live calls only.
remove_ro_timer() unlinks from either list as before, and the timer routine
takes what expired from both.

